// A boundary-call parameter whose declared type is a fixed array of PRIMITIVE
// elements has no lowering route, and neither does such a result. Reduced from
// three GUI samples that all stop at the same call,
// `self.gui.window_create(self.cls, self.title, ...)`, whose boundary declares
// `class_name: [u8; 7], title: [u8; 10]`:
// `gui/{window_app, window_demo, image_viewer}`.
//
//   machine take(&mut self, values: [u8; 4])        OMITTED (this fixture)
//   machine take(&mut self, value: u8)              checks   (the near-twin)
//   machine take(&mut self, values: &mut [u8; 4])   checks   (a reference)
//   machine take(&mut self, values: [i32; 4096])    OMITTED
//
// at `call operation: boundary arguments`; a boundary machine RETURNING such
// an array declines symmetrically at `call operation: boundary result`.
//
// Nothing about the call site matters -- not the element type (`u8`, `i32`,
// `u64` alike), not the length (1, 4, 6, 7, 4096 alike), not the argument
// count (eight scalar arguments pass), not the argument's provenance (a self
// field and a byte-string literal both decline), and not the return direction
// (a void-returning boundary machine declines the same way). Declaring such a
// method and never CALLING it is fine. Only the declared parameter type at a
// boundary call matters.
//
// It is narrower than "any composite by value". A record by value, and
// `[Point; 2]` by value, also fail at a boundary -- but at a DIFFERENT phase,
// `completion`, which is a separate decline site not isolated here. A
// composite by value at an ORDINARY machine call passes. So the samples' gap
// is specifically a fixed-array-of-primitive at a boundary call.
//
// The guards are unconditional and read directly:
// `04_typed-trees-to-checked-trees/src/execution/terminal_unit/calls/trait_boundary_call.rs`
// has `if validation::is_closed_primitive_array_type(program, formal) { return
// None; }` inside the loop opened by the `boundary arguments` phase, and the
// same guard on the substituted return type just after the `boundary result`
// phase. `is_closed_primitive_array_type` is true exactly for a literal-length
// `FixedArray` whose possibly-nested element is `Bool | I8..I64 | U8..U64 |
// F32 | F64`, which matches the measured predicate including why `[Point; 2]`
// escapes it and declines later instead. The argument guard sits BEFORE the
// byte-sequence-literal and subslice admissions, which is why a string-literal
// argument declines too.
//
// Untested prediction from that code, worth one line when repairing: a nested
// `[[u8; 2]; 2]` should decline identically.
//
// Why the samples look inconsistent: `image_viewer`'s
// `self.raw.read(self.fd, &mut self.buffer, 16384)` already checks, because it
// passes a REFERENCE. Converting every array-typed boundary parameter in
// `window_app` and `window_demo` to `&mut` makes both check clean; doing it
// for `image_viewer` clears the omission and leaves only honest
// provider-absence diagnostics.
use omega::language::core::binding;

pub boundary trait Thing {
    machine take(&mut self, values: [u8; 4]) -> u64;
}

data ThingProvider {
    code: u64;
}

macos_arm64 machine ThingProvider::provider_defaults(defaults: &mut ThingProvider) {
    defaults.select_provider<Thing, ThingProvider>();
}

macos_arm64 machine ThingProvider::take(&mut self, values: [u8; 4]) -> u64 satisfies Thing::take {
    self.code = 7;
    transition { _ -> ret() }
    state ret(&mut self) -> u64 { self.code }
}

data Main {
    thing: Binding<Thing>;
    buffer: [u8; 4];
    result: u64;
}

machine Main::main(&mut self) reaches Thing {
    self.buffer[0] = 1;
    self.result = self.thing.take(self.buffer);
}
